<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=sulfonamide">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sulfonamide</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1932">1932</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as sulfa drugs, sulphonamides, sulpha drugs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sulfonamides, or sulfa drugs, were the first broadly effective antibacterial medicines used systemically. Experiments began in 1932 in the laboratories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayer">Bayer</a> AG, then part of IG Farben, where a team led by Gerhard Domagk found that Prontosil, a red dye synthesised by Josef Klarer, stopped some bacterial infections in mice; the first report appeared in 1935. A team at the Pasteur Institute showed that the body breaks Prontosil down into sulfanilamide, a compound first made in 1906 whose patent had expired. Between 1937 and 1943 sulfa drugs lowered maternal mortality in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> by 25–40%.</p></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1932</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Bayer AG laboratories</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Gerhard Domagk · Josef Klarer · Fritz Mietzsch</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Domagk's team found the drug, but its active compound, sulfanilamide, had been synthesised in 1906 and used in dye-making; a French team at the Pasteur Institute showed that Prontosil works by releasing it in the body.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/germ-theory-of-disease">Germ theory of disease<span>1658</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/synthetic-dye">Synthetic dye<span>1856</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=sulfonamide">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
